WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:07.220
THE PRESIDENT: Hello, Maryland!

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:08.690
(Applause.)

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:11.630
Hello, PGCC!

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.100
(Applause.)

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:24.869
It's good to be back in PG County.

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:26.340
(Applause.)

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:32.891
Give it up for one of the hardest-working,
most effective governors in the country, Governor

00:00:32.891 --> 00:00:34.640
Martin O'Malley.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:36.059
(Applause.)

00:00:36.059 --> 00:00:42.370
Well, it is great to be with all of you here
today --

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:48.140
AUDIENCE MEMBER: I love you!

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.140
THE PRESIDENT: I love you back.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:50.140
It's wonderful being here.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.140
(Applause.)

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:56.320
We also have a few folks who work so hard
on behalf of the people of Maryland every

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.430
single day: Senator Ben Cardin is here.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.020
(Applause.)

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.859
Congressman Steny Hoyer is here.

00:01:06.859 --> 00:01:07.859
(Applause.)

00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:12.149
And Congresswoman Donna Edwards is here.

00:01:12.149 --> 00:01:14.270
(Applause.)

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.139
And all of you are here.

00:01:16.139 --> 00:01:17.429
(Applause.)

00:01:17.429 --> 00:01:25.630
Sometimes you just need to escape Beltway
politics for a little bit -- even if you're

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:29.319
just a mile or two outside the Beltway.

00:01:29.319 --> 00:01:40.649
Now, I know that a lot of you have seen some
of the antics going on in Congress right now.

00:01:40.649 --> 00:01:45.310
(Laughter.)

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:52.880
So I wanted to take a little bit of time today
to speak with you -- the people who send us

00:01:52.880 --> 00:02:01.029
to serve -- about something that is critical
to our families, critical to our businesses,

00:02:01.029 --> 00:02:03.319
critical to our economy.

00:02:03.319 --> 00:02:08.039
And that is the reforms that we are making
to our health care system.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:17.329
There's been a lot of things said, a lot of
misinformation, a lot of confusion.

00:02:17.329 --> 00:02:22.819
But there are few things more fundamental
to the economic security of the middle class

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:29.860
and everybody who's trying to get into the
middle class than health care.

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:38.719
For a long time, America was the only advanced
economy in the world where health care was

00:02:38.719 --> 00:02:42.769
not a right, but a privilege.

00:02:42.769 --> 00:02:46.689
We spent more, we got less.

00:02:46.689 --> 00:02:53.080
We left tens of millions of Americans without
the security of health insurance.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:59.670
By the time the financial crisis hit, most
folks' premiums had more than doubled in about

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.620
a decade.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:08.560
About one in 10 Americans who got their health
care through their employer lost that coverage.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.030
So the health care system was not working.

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:21.420
And the rising costs of health care burdened
businesses and became the biggest driver of

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:25.530
our long-term deficits.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:31.099
But this has always been about more than just
statistics.

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:42.030
Everywhere I went as I ran for President back
in 2007, 2008, everyplace I've gone as President,

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:48.569
I would hear stories from folks just like
you of insurers that denied a child coverage

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:54.599
because he had a preexisting condition like
asthma; of cancer survivors that had to choose

00:03:54.599 --> 00:04:00.310
between their home or their health care; of
small businesses who wanted to do the right

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:06.180
thing by their employees but had seen their
insurance premiums go up so high that they

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.159
just couldn't do it anymore.

00:04:10.159 --> 00:04:15.579
And these stories were personal for me, because
I remember my mother worrying about how she

00:04:15.579 --> 00:04:18.949
was going to deal with her finances when she
got very sick.

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:24.380
I remember the fear Michelle and I felt when
Sasha was a few months old and she got meningitis.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:28.220
And we raced to the hospital and they had
to give her a spinal tap.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.650
And we didn't know what was wrong and we were
terrified, never felt so scared or helpless

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:34.650
in all of my life.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:39.040
But we were fortunate enough to have good
health insurance.

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.180
And I remember looking around that emergency
room and thinking -- what about the parents

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.900
who aren't that lucky?

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:51.190
What about the parents who get hit with a
bill of $20,000 or $30,000 and they've got

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:54.430
no idea how to pay for it?

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:58.199
What about those parents whose kids have a
chronic illness like asthma and have to keep

00:04:58.199 --> 00:05:02.641
on going back to the emergency room because
they don't have a regular doctor, and the

00:05:02.641 --> 00:05:04.160
bills never stop coming?

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:09.259
Who is going to stand up for them?

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:17.900
In the wealthiest nation on Earth, no one
should go broke just because they get sick.

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:20.610
(Applause.)

00:05:20.610 --> 00:05:30.030
In the United States of America, health care
is not a privilege for the fortunate few -- it

00:05:30.030 --> 00:05:31.660
is a right.

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:36.500
And I knew that if we didn't do something
about our unfair and inefficient health care

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:43.389
system, it would keep driving up our deficits,
it would keep burdening our businesses, it

00:05:43.389 --> 00:05:50.520
would keep hurting our families, and it would
keep holding back economic growth.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.449
That's why we took on a broken health care
system.

00:05:54.449 --> 00:06:03.770
That's why, with the help of folks like Steny
and Ben and Donna, we got it through Congress.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.069
That's why we've been implementing it.

00:06:06.069 --> 00:06:08.349
That's why we are going to see it through.

00:06:08.349 --> 00:06:11.789
The Affordable Care Act is here.

00:06:11.789 --> 00:06:12.849
(Applause.)

00:06:12.849 --> 00:06:22.060
I don't have to tell you it was a challenge
to get it done.

00:06:22.060 --> 00:06:23.060
(Laughter.)

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:31.720
A lot of special interests who liked the system
just the way it was fought us tooth and nail.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:38.180
Then Republicans decided it was good politics
to fight it, even though the plan we proposed

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:40.860
drew on a lot of Republican ideas.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:51.770
But despite all the obstacles, the Affordable
Care Act passed both houses of Congress.

00:06:51.770 --> 00:06:58.949
I signed it into law.

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:02.129
(Applause.)

00:07:02.129 --> 00:07:08.310
The Supreme Court ruled it constitutional.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:09.490
(Applause.)

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:19.449
Republicans in Congress have now voted more
than 40 times to undermine or repeal it.

00:07:19.449 --> 00:07:26.200
Their candidate for president ran on a platform
to repeal it.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:31.659
And at every step, they've been unsuccessful.

00:07:31.659 --> 00:07:34.659
(Applause.)

00:07:34.659 --> 00:07:48.300
Now, five days from now -- five days from
now -- on October 1st, millions of Americans

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:53.800
who don't have health insurance because they've
been priced out of the market or because they've

00:07:53.800 --> 00:08:00.659
been denied access because of a preexisting
condition, they will finally be able to buy

00:08:00.659 --> 00:08:04.680
quality, affordable health insurance.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:06.320
(Applause.)

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:11.240
In five days.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.880
(Applause.)

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:18.870
Preexisting conditions, whether it's back
pain or allergies that were sticking you with

00:08:18.870 --> 00:08:24.300
sky-high premiums, those no longer will prevent
you from getting affordable coverage that

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.849
you need.

00:08:26.849 --> 00:08:28.800
That's going to happen in five days.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:38.550
Now, of course, the closer we've gotten to
this date, the more irresponsible folks who

00:08:38.550 --> 00:08:44.580
are opposed to this law have become.

00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:50.370
Some of the same Republicans who warned three
years ago that this law would be "Armageddon"

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:59.520
-- that's what they said -- "Armageddon" -- now
they're threatening steps that actually would

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:03.711
badly hurt our entire economy -- not because
of the Affordable Care Act but because of

00:09:03.711 --> 00:09:06.200
what they're threatening to do.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.980
Some have threatened a government shutdown
if they can't shut down this law.

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:15.890
Others have actually threatened an economic
shutdown by refusing to pay America's bills

00:09:15.890 --> 00:09:18.430
if they can't delay the law.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:19.800
AUDIENCE: Booo --

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:30.780
THE PRESIDENT: That's not going to happen
as long as I'm President.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:32.580
(Applause.)

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:36.540
The Affordable Care Act is here to stay.

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.840
(Applause.)

00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:46.260
And so today, I want to speak plainly, clearly,
honestly, about what it means for you and

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.110
for the people you care about.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:56.000
Now, let's start with the fact that even before
the Affordable Care Act fully takes effect,

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:00.220
about 85 percent of Americans already have
health insurance --- either through their

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:04.510
job, or through Medicare, or through the individual
market.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:10.450
So if you're one of these folks, it's reasonable
that you might worry whether health care reform

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:16.190
is going to create changes that are a problem
for you -- especially when you're bombarded

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:18.810
with all sorts of fear-mongering.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:23.700
So the first thing you need to know is this:
If you already have health care, you don't

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.060
have to do anything.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.890
In fact, for the past few years, since I signed
the Affordable Care Act, a lot of you have

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:36.330
been enjoying new benefits and protections
that you didn't before even if you didn't

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:41.700
know they were coming from Obamacare.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:45.540
(Applause.)

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:48.220
Let me just give you a few examples.

00:10:48.220 --> 00:10:52.580
Because of the Affordable Care Act, more than
100 million Americans have gotten free preventive

00:10:52.580 --> 00:10:58.080
care like mammograms and contraceptive care
with no copays.

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.120
(Applause.)

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:05.200
Because of the Affordable Care Act, 3 million
young adults under age 26 have gained coverage

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:13.070
by staying on their parents' plan.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:14.070
(Applause.)

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:17.080
Because of the Affordable Care Act, millions
of seniors on Medicare have saved hundreds

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.510
of dollars on their prescription medicine.

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:23.130
They've been getting their prescription drugs
cheaper.

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.330
(Applause.)

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:30.760
Because of the Affordable Care Act, just this
year, 8.5 million families actually got an

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:35.940
average of $100 back from their insurance
companies because the insurance companies

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:43.860
spent too much on things like overhead, and
not enough on actual Medicare -- medical care.

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.170
(Applause.)

00:11:46.170 --> 00:11:49.990
Because of the Affordable Care Act, insurance
companies can no longer put lifetime limits

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:55.960
on the care your family needs, or discriminate
against children with preexisting conditions.

00:11:55.960 --> 00:12:02.600
And starting on January 1st, they won't be
able to charge women more for their insurance

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.280
just because they're women.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.820
(Applause.)

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:13.000
That's a good thing.

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:22.640
So tens of millions of Americans are already
better off because of the benefits and protections

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:24.910
provided by the Affordable Care Act.

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:30.070
Like I said, they may not know why that rebate
check came in the mail.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:31.440
(Laughter.)

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:37.950
They may not notice that they're not having
to copay for some preventive care that they

00:12:37.950 --> 00:12:40.040
received.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:41.730
But they're getting those benefits.

00:12:41.730 --> 00:12:43.060
That's already happening.

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.060
That's already in place today.

00:12:44.060 --> 00:12:45.820
It's been going on for several years.

00:12:45.820 --> 00:12:52.980
Those are the benefits of Obamacare -- the
law that Republicans want to repeal.

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:57.420
Although it's interesting -- when you ask
Republicans whether they'd repeal the benefits

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:01.420
I just mentioned, when you say to them, well,
do you think it's the right thing to do to

00:13:01.420 --> 00:13:06.440
let young people stay on their parents' plans
so they can keep insurance, or do you want

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:12.360
to prevent seniors from getting more discounts
on their prescription drugs, then they'll

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.790
say, no, no, no, we like those.

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.900
Those things are okay.

00:13:16.900 --> 00:13:18.600
(Laughter.)

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:24.460
So they don't like Obamacare in theory, but
some of the component parts, at least those

00:13:24.460 --> 00:13:28.260
that poll well, they don't mind.

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.430
But that's already in place.

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:34.480
Now, here's the second thing you need to know.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.870
If you're one of over 40 million Americans
who don't have health insurance --- including

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:45.061
hundreds of thousands of folks right here
in Maryland --- starting on Tuesday, five

00:13:45.061 --> 00:13:52.370
days from now, you'll finally have the same
chance to buy quality, affordable health care

00:13:52.370 --> 00:13:53.440
as everybody else.

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:56.220
AUDIENCE MEMBER: Thanks, Mr. President!

00:13:56.220 --> 00:13:57.220
(Applause.)

00:13:57.220 --> 00:14:01.451
THE PRESIDENT: And I want to break this down
for you.

00:14:01.451 --> 00:14:04.640
I want you to know exactly how it works.

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:11.160
The major reason why people don't have health
insurance is either they don't have a job,

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:18.500
or they do have a job but their employer doesn't
offer health insurance, or they're self-employed.

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:23.020
If you've ever tried to buy health insurance
on your own, you know it is really, really

00:14:23.020 --> 00:14:24.020
expensive.

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:25.260
AUDIENCE: Yes!

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:30.860
THE PRESIDENT: It's even worse if you have
a preexisting condition --- and up to half

00:14:30.860 --> 00:14:33.870
of all Americans have a preexisting condition.

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:39.920
See, the reason it's really expensive if you're
buying it on your own is because you're not

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:44.920
part of a big group, you're not part of a
group plan.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:49.500
And what groups do is they spread risk between
sick and healthy people, between older and

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:51.780
younger people.

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:55.442
And groups -- because insurance companies
want the business of groups -- that's a lot

00:14:55.442 --> 00:15:01.240
of customers -- they'll negotiate a better
deal with a group than they will with an individual.

00:15:01.240 --> 00:15:04.420
So if you're on your own, you're out there
trying to negotiate with an insurance company,

00:15:04.420 --> 00:15:08.640
they're looking and they're saying, well,
you take it or leave it, I'm going to charge

00:15:08.640 --> 00:15:10.710
you a whole lot of money.

00:15:10.710 --> 00:15:13.910
And if you've got a preexisting condition,
they'll say, we don't even want to insure

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:18.520
you because we think you might get sick later
on and we don't really want to pay, we just

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.800
want to take in premiums.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:28.260
So if you're not part of a group, you're either
uninsurable, or you need to spend a small

00:15:28.260 --> 00:15:32.460
fortune on insurance that oftentimes is not
very good.

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:36.790
That's what's happening right now.

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:40.100
The Affordable Care Act was designed to solve
that problem.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:41.750
And here's how we do it.

00:15:41.750 --> 00:15:50.700
Starting on Tuesday, every American can visit
HealthCare.gov to find out what's called the

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:53.910
insurance marketplace for your state.

00:15:53.910 --> 00:15:57.100
Here in Maryland, I actually think it's called
MarylandHealthConnection.gov.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:15:58.100
(Applause.)

00:15:58.100 --> 00:15:59.100
MarylandHealthConnection.gov.

00:15:59.100 --> 00:16:08.550
But if you go to HealthCare.gov, you can look
and they'll tell you where to go.

00:16:08.550 --> 00:16:10.580
They'll link to your state.

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:13.540
Now, this is real simple.

00:16:13.540 --> 00:16:20.920
It's a website where you can compare and purchase
affordable health insurance plans, side-by-side,

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:25.560
the same way you shop for a plane ticket on
Kayak -- (laughter) -- same way you shop for

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.140
a TV on Amazon.

00:16:29.140 --> 00:16:35.410
You just go on and you start looking, and
here are all the options.

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:40.720
It's buying insurance on the private market,
but because now you're part of a big group

00:16:40.720 --> 00:16:47.670
plan -- everybody in Maryland is all logging
in and taking a look at the prices -- you've

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.380
got new choices.

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:53.030
Now you've got new competition, because insurers
want your business.

00:16:53.030 --> 00:16:56.250
And that means you will have cheaper prices.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:57.250
(Applause.)

00:16:57.250 --> 00:17:04.670
So you enter in some basic information about
yourself, what level of coverage you're looking

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:05.670
for.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:10.379
After that, you'll be presented with a list
of quality, affordable plans that are available

00:17:10.379 --> 00:17:11.909
in your area.

00:17:11.909 --> 00:17:17.009
It will say clearly what each plan covers,
what each plan costs.

00:17:17.009 --> 00:17:18.750
The price will be right there.

00:17:18.750 --> 00:17:20.970
It will be fully transparent.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:25.110
Before this law, only a handful of states
required insurance companies to offer you

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:29.130
instant price quotes, but because of this
law, insurers in all 50 states will have to

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:32.909
offer you instant price quotes.

00:17:32.909 --> 00:17:39.390
And so if you've ever tried to buy insurance
on your own, I promise you this is a lot easier.

00:17:39.390 --> 00:17:45.460
It's like booking a hotel or a plane ticket.

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:49.490
And here's another thing about these new plans.

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:56.520
If you're one of those folks who has a preexisting
condition, these plans have to offer you coverage.

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:01.590
They can't use your medical history to charge
you more than anybody else.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:07.620
If you couldn't afford coverage for your child
because he had asthma, he's covered.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:12.340
If you couldn't afford coverage because you
were told heartburn was a preexisting condition,

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:13.950
you're covered.

00:18:13.950 --> 00:18:14.950
(Laughter.)

00:18:14.950 --> 00:18:20.690
If you're one of the 45 million Americans
with a mental illness, you are covered.

00:18:20.690 --> 00:18:29.159
If you're a young adult or entrepreneur striking
out on your own, you're covered.

00:18:29.159 --> 00:18:30.860
(Applause.)

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:36.110
If you're a young couple who previously had
insurance that didn't include maternity benefits

00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:42.519
and now suddenly you need some maternity benefits,
you're covered.

00:18:42.519 --> 00:18:43.720
(Applause.)

00:18:43.720 --> 00:18:54.210
If you lose your job and your health care
with it, you're covered.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:18:55.980
(Applause.)

00:18:55.980 --> 00:19:01.120
So all those things that would deny you coverage
in the past, that were the cruelties of a

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:06.961
broken health care system, on January 1st,
when these plans take effect --

00:19:06.961 --> 00:19:07.961
AUDIENCE MEMBER: October 1st.

00:19:07.961 --> 00:19:09.250
THE PRESIDENT: No, no, no, no -- hold on.

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:10.250
(Laughter.)

00:19:10.250 --> 00:19:11.250
Hold on.

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:12.250
I know what I'm talking about.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:13.419
You sign up starting on Tuesday.

00:19:13.419 --> 00:19:14.419
(Laughter.)

00:19:14.419 --> 00:19:19.139
The plan will take effect on January 1st.

00:19:19.139 --> 00:19:23.230
And when these plans take effect, all those
things change forever.

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:29.190
Now, what about choice and cost?

00:19:29.190 --> 00:19:34.190
In states where the federal government helps
run these marketplaces, the average American

00:19:34.190 --> 00:19:40.030
will have more than 50 different plans to
choose from, with different levels of coverage.

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:43.830
And because insurance companies are competing
against one another for your business, a lot

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:50.760
of Americans will pay significantly less for
their insurance than they do now.

00:19:50.760 --> 00:19:53.440
Premiums are going to be different in different
parts of the country, depending on how much

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:54.630
coverage you buy.

00:19:54.630 --> 00:20:01.990
But 95 percent of uninsured Americans will
see their premiums cost less than was expected.

00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:06.050
And many families, including more than two-thirds
of all young adults who buy health care through

00:20:06.050 --> 00:20:11.039
these online marketplaces, are also going
to be eligible for tax credits that bring

00:20:11.039 --> 00:20:15.539
down the cost down even further.

00:20:15.539 --> 00:20:19.279
(Applause.)

00:20:19.279 --> 00:20:20.999
So let me be specific.

00:20:20.999 --> 00:20:26.999
Right here in Maryland, average 25-year-old
-- have we got any 25-year-olds here?

00:20:26.999 --> 00:20:27.999
(Applause.)

00:20:27.999 --> 00:20:31.669
All right, so we've got a few.

00:20:31.669 --> 00:20:37.000
Some of you raised your hand -- I'm not sure
you're -- (laughter.)

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:44.860
Here in Maryland, average 25-year-old making
$25,000 a year could end up getting covered

00:20:44.860 --> 00:20:48.889
for as little as $80 a month -- $80 a month.

00:20:48.889 --> 00:20:53.340
(Applause.)

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:58.370
Here in Maryland, a family of four making
$60,000 a year could get covered for as little

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:05.850
as $164 a month.

00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:08.540
It's the same story across the country.

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:15.059
In Texas, average 27-year-old making $25,000
could get covered for as little as $83 a month.

00:21:15.059 --> 00:21:19.259
In Florida, a family of four making $50,000
could get covered for as little as $104 a

00:21:19.259 --> 00:21:20.259
month.

00:21:20.259 --> 00:21:24.250
And keep in mind the government didn't set
these prices.

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:31.340
The insurance companies -- they proposed these
prices because they want to get in with these

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:34.110
big groups, with all these new customers.

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:38.730
The insurance companies are saying these marketplaces,
this law, will work.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:42.850
They're putting money on the line because
they think it will work.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:49.860
Competition, choice, transparency -- all these
things are keeping costs down.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:53.130
Knowing you can offer your family the security
of health care -- that's priceless.

00:21:53.130 --> 00:21:56.809
Now you can do it for the cost of your cable
bill.

00:21:56.809 --> 00:22:00.830
Probably less than your cellphone bill.

00:22:00.830 --> 00:22:09.090
(Laughter and applause.)

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:10.090
Think about that.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:17.669
Good health insurance for the price of your
cellphone bill, or less.

00:22:17.669 --> 00:22:23.230
And let's say you're a young woman, you just
turned -- I'm interested in this, because

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:25.179
I got two daughters, right?

00:22:25.179 --> 00:22:28.159
Let's say you just turned 26.

00:22:28.159 --> 00:22:31.390
Let's say you can't stay on your parents'
plan anymore.

00:22:31.390 --> 00:22:37.289
If you buy health care through the marketplace,
your plan has to cover free checkups, flu

00:22:37.289 --> 00:22:40.429
shots, contraceptive care.

00:22:40.429 --> 00:22:48.780
So you might end up getting more health care
each month than you're paying for the premiums.

00:22:48.780 --> 00:22:53.850
All told, nearly 6 in 10 Americans without
health insurance today will be able to get

00:22:53.850 --> 00:22:56.460
covered for $100 or less.

00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:01.549
It would actually be 8 in 10 if every governor
were working as hard as Governor O'Malley

00:23:01.549 --> 00:23:07.929
to make the Affordable Care Act work for their
citizens.

00:23:07.929 --> 00:23:09.220
(Applause.)

00:23:09.220 --> 00:23:16.309
Unfortunately, we've still got a few Republican
governors who are so opposed to the very idea

00:23:16.309 --> 00:23:21.690
of the law -- or at least they're doing it
for politics -- that they haven't lifted a

00:23:21.690 --> 00:23:23.940
finger to help cover more people.

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:28.639
Some of them have actually tried to harm the
law before it takes effect.

00:23:28.639 --> 00:23:35.529
But a lot of Republican governors are putting
politics aside and doing the right thing.

00:23:35.529 --> 00:23:36.529
(Applause.)

00:23:36.529 --> 00:23:38.460
And they deserve congratulations for that.

00:23:38.460 --> 00:23:39.460
It wasn't easy for them.

00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:46.139
But you've got conservative governors in Ohio,
in Michigan, in Pennsylvania and Arizona -- about

00:23:46.139 --> 00:23:50.309
eight Republican governors in all, they've
decided to expand Medicaid through the Affordable

00:23:50.309 --> 00:23:53.919
Care Act to cover more people in their states.

00:23:53.919 --> 00:23:59.220
And millions of Americans without insurance
will get coverage through these programs.

00:23:59.220 --> 00:24:03.039
So that's what the Affordable Care Act is.

00:24:03.039 --> 00:24:05.629
That's what all the fuss is about.

00:24:05.629 --> 00:24:10.700
We're giving more benefits and protections
for folks who already have health insurance,

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:16.509
and we created a new market -- basically a
big group plan -- for folks without health

00:24:16.509 --> 00:24:21.639
insurance so that they get a better deal,
and then we're providing tax credits to help

00:24:21.639 --> 00:24:23.510
folks afford it.

00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:28.580
You would think that would not be so controversial.

00:24:28.580 --> 00:24:30.269
(Laughter.)

00:24:30.269 --> 00:24:36.120
You would think people would say, okay, let's
go ahead and let's do this so everybody has

00:24:36.120 --> 00:24:39.120
health insurance coverage.

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:46.340
The result is more choice, more competition,
real health care security.

00:24:46.340 --> 00:24:51.100
And one question people ask: How is it possible
to do all this and keep costs down?

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:57.519
Well, part of what we did was build into the
law all sorts of measures to assure that the

00:24:57.519 --> 00:25:00.600
growth of health care costs would start slowing
down.

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:01.600
And it has.

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:08.860
See, under the old system, doctors and hospitals,
they were rewarded not for the quality of

00:25:08.860 --> 00:25:10.159
care, but for the quantity of care.

00:25:10.159 --> 00:25:14.570
They'd get paid for the number of procedures
they did instead of whether they were working

00:25:14.570 --> 00:25:15.570
or not.

00:25:15.570 --> 00:25:21.169
Now, there are penalties for hospitals with
high readmission rates.

00:25:21.169 --> 00:25:26.799
And last year, surprisingly enough, for the
first time ever, hospital readmission rates

00:25:26.799 --> 00:25:31.240
for Medicare patients actually fell.

00:25:31.240 --> 00:25:34.000
(Applause.)

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:36.769
Right?

00:25:36.769 --> 00:25:41.419
That means fewer taxpayer dollars go to providers
that don't serve their patients well.

00:25:41.419 --> 00:25:46.769
Over the past five years, we've more than
doubled the adoption of electronic health

00:25:46.769 --> 00:25:48.309
records for physicians.

00:25:48.309 --> 00:25:53.100
So that means they can track what's going
on better and make fewer mistakes.

00:25:53.100 --> 00:25:57.470
New technology startup companies are coming
up with new inventions to monitor patient

00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:59.850
health, prevent infections.

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:02.630
There's innovation going on all across the
country.

00:26:02.630 --> 00:26:09.230
As a consequence, today, Medicare costs per
enrollee are rising at the slowest rate in

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:10.660
years.

00:26:10.660 --> 00:26:15.379
Employer-based health care costs are growing
at about one-third the rate of a decade ago.

00:26:15.379 --> 00:26:21.450
All told, since I signed the Affordable Care
Act into law, we have seen the slowest growth

00:26:21.450 --> 00:26:33.620
in health care costs on record.

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:34.620
(Applause.)

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:35.889
So let's think about this.

00:26:35.889 --> 00:26:39.200
If you got health insurance, you're getting
better protections, better benefits.

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.760
If you don't have health insurance, you're
now getting to be part of a group plan.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:51.520
And health care costs overall are rising much
more slowly than they did before we signed

00:26:51.520 --> 00:26:52.720
the law.

00:26:52.720 --> 00:26:54.280
So far, so good.

00:26:54.280 --> 00:26:58.120
So what's all the fuss about?

00:26:58.120 --> 00:27:03.149
What is it that everybody -- what is that
these Republicans are just so mad about?

00:27:03.149 --> 00:27:04.210
AUDIENCE: Booo --

00:27:04.210 --> 00:27:09.509
THE PRESIDENT: No, no, no, look, I want to
be honest.

00:27:09.509 --> 00:27:16.100
There are parts of the bill that some folks
don't like.

00:27:16.100 --> 00:27:21.179
To help pay for the program, the wealthiest
Americans --- families who make more than

00:27:21.179 --> 00:27:28.059
$250,000 a year --- will have to pay a little
bit more.

00:27:28.059 --> 00:27:38.539
Extremely costly health insurance plans will
no longer qualify for unlimited tax breaks.

00:27:38.539 --> 00:27:46.490
And most people who can afford health insurance
now have to take responsibility to buy health

00:27:46.490 --> 00:27:48.749
insurance, or pay a penalty.

00:27:48.749 --> 00:27:49.960
(Applause.)

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:51.169
Right?

00:27:51.169 --> 00:27:58.460
Now, the reason we do that is, when uninsured
people who can afford to get health insurance

00:27:58.460 --> 00:28:03.600
don't, and then they get sick or they get
hit by a car, and they show up at the emergency

00:28:03.600 --> 00:28:05.870
room, who do you think pays for that?

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:06.870
AUDIENCE: We do.

00:28:06.870 --> 00:28:10.320
THE PRESIDENT: You do, in the form of higher
premiums.

00:28:10.320 --> 00:28:14.700
Because the hospitals, they've got to get
their money back somehow.

00:28:14.700 --> 00:28:18.909
So if they're treating somebody who doesn't
have health insurance, they jack up premiums

00:28:18.909 --> 00:28:20.950
for everybody who does have health insurance.

00:28:20.950 --> 00:28:26.529
It's like a hidden tax of $1,000 per family
every year who has got health insurance.

00:28:26.529 --> 00:28:28.169
So we're saying that's not fair.

00:28:28.169 --> 00:28:33.110
If you can afford to get health insurance,
don't dump the costs on us.

00:28:33.110 --> 00:28:38.539
The law also requires employers with more
than 50 employees to either provide health

00:28:38.539 --> 00:28:41.769
insurance for your workers or pay a penalty.

00:28:41.769 --> 00:28:45.529
Now, some folks say, well, that's not fair.

00:28:45.529 --> 00:28:52.770
But if you are an employer, you can afford
to provide health insurance, you don't, your

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:58.940
employees get sick, they go to the emergency
room or they end up on Medicaid because you're

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:03.710
not doing what you're doing -- you should
be doing -- why is it everybody else should

00:29:03.710 --> 00:29:05.610
be bearing those costs?

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:08.850
Now, there are some folks who disagree with
me on this.

00:29:08.850 --> 00:29:12.869
They say that violates people's liberty, telling
them they've got to get health insurance.

00:29:12.869 --> 00:29:15.450
Well, I disagree.

00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:18.580
So did Congress when it passed this bill into
law.

00:29:18.580 --> 00:29:27.259
It is unfair for folks to game the system
and make the rest of us pay for it.

00:29:27.259 --> 00:29:31.029
(Applause.)

00:29:31.029 --> 00:29:35.749
It's unfair for responsible employers who
are doing the right thing, giving their employees

00:29:35.749 --> 00:29:42.519
health insurance, to get undercut by some
operator that's not providing health insurance

00:29:42.519 --> 00:29:43.970
for their employees.

00:29:43.970 --> 00:29:49.659
That puts the employer who's doing the right
thing at a disadvantage, right?

00:29:49.659 --> 00:29:54.690
So this idea that you've got responsibilities
-- everybody -- that's what Massachusetts

00:29:54.690 --> 00:29:57.470
did when they passed their health care plan
a few years ago.

00:29:57.470 --> 00:30:01.900
And, by the way, today, in Massachusetts,
almost everybody is covered and the system

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:05.620
works pretty well.

00:30:05.620 --> 00:30:07.389
(Applause.)

00:30:07.389 --> 00:30:16.610
All right, let me just wrap up by saying this:
Like any law, like any big product launch,

00:30:16.610 --> 00:30:22.570
there are going to be some glitches as this
thing unfolds.

00:30:22.570 --> 00:30:25.119
Folks in different parts of the country will
have different experiences.

00:30:25.119 --> 00:30:28.970
It's going to be smoother in places like Maryland
where governors are working to implement it

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:32.110
rather than fight it.

00:30:32.110 --> 00:30:34.540
(Applause.)

00:30:34.540 --> 00:30:41.480
But somewhere around the country, there's
going to be a computer glitch and the website's

00:30:41.480 --> 00:30:46.200
not working quite the way it's supposed to,
or something happens where there's some error

00:30:46.200 --> 00:30:48.330
made somewhere -- that will happen.

00:30:48.330 --> 00:30:51.369
That happens whenever you roll out a new program.

00:30:51.369 --> 00:30:56.230
And I guarantee you, the opponents of the
law, they'll have their cameras ready to document

00:30:56.230 --> 00:31:00.539
anything that doesn't go completely right,
and they'll send it to the news folks and

00:31:00.539 --> 00:31:03.049
they'll say, look at this, this thing is not
working.

00:31:03.049 --> 00:31:08.360
But most of the stories you'll hear about
how Obamacare just can't work is just not

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:10.679
based on facts.

00:31:10.679 --> 00:31:18.859
Every time they have predicted something not
working, it's worked.

00:31:18.859 --> 00:31:20.720
(Applause.)

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:28.980
I mean, they said that these rates would come
in real high and everybody's premiums would

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:29.980
be sky high.

00:31:29.980 --> 00:31:34.340
And it turns out, lo and behold, actually,
the prices came in lower than we expected

00:31:34.340 --> 00:31:36.460
-- lower than I predicted.

00:31:36.460 --> 00:31:38.669
That's how well competition and choice work.

00:31:38.669 --> 00:31:39.669
(Applause.)

00:31:39.669 --> 00:31:44.259
They said this would be a disaster in terms
of jobs.

00:31:44.259 --> 00:31:48.169
There's no widespread evidence that the Affordable
Care Act is hurting jobs.

00:31:48.169 --> 00:31:53.309
One of John McCain's former economic advisors
admitted just this week -- and I'm quoting

00:31:53.309 --> 00:31:57.019
here -- "I was expecting to see it.

00:31:57.019 --> 00:32:00.629
I was looking for it," but "it's not there."

00:32:00.629 --> 00:32:04.769
It's not there.

00:32:04.769 --> 00:32:07.590
(Applause.)

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:12.720
The reason is reforming health care is going
to help the economy over the long term.

00:32:12.720 --> 00:32:18.210
Not only will it help lower costs for businesses,
not only will it help families, it will free

00:32:18.210 --> 00:32:20.379
up entrepreneurship in this country.

00:32:20.379 --> 00:32:26.019
Because if you've got a great idea for your
own business but you've never tried it because

00:32:26.019 --> 00:32:29.809
your spouse had a preexisting condition and
you didn't want to lose your employer-based

00:32:29.809 --> 00:32:34.159
coverage, you've got the ability now to get
your own coverage.

00:32:34.159 --> 00:32:35.419
That's security.

00:32:35.419 --> 00:32:37.480
That's freedom.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:41.870
So we're now only five days away from finishing
the job.

00:32:41.870 --> 00:32:44.539
AUDIENCE MEMBER: Five days away.

00:32:44.539 --> 00:32:50.039
THE PRESIDENT: Five days.

00:32:50.039 --> 00:32:51.419
(Applause.)

00:32:51.419 --> 00:32:54.840
Starting on Tuesday, you can sign up.

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:57.359
But you don't have to sign up on Tuesday.

00:32:57.359 --> 00:33:00.759
You've got six months to enroll in these new
plans.

00:33:00.759 --> 00:33:08.879
You can go to the website; you can check it
out; you can see if what I'm saying is true.

00:33:08.879 --> 00:33:10.960
(Laughter.)

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:13.000
You can sign up next week.

00:33:13.000 --> 00:33:14.429
You can sign up next month.

00:33:14.429 --> 00:33:17.989
You can sign up two months from now, three
months from now.

00:33:17.989 --> 00:33:20.100
But you can sign up.

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:26.200
Tell your friends, tell your classmates, tell
your family members about the new health care

00:33:26.200 --> 00:33:27.240
choices.

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:32.330
Talk to folks at your church, in your classroom.

00:33:32.330 --> 00:33:35.679
You're going to a football game, basketball
game -- talk to them.

00:33:35.679 --> 00:33:36.989
Tell them what the law means.

00:33:36.989 --> 00:33:41.559
And over the next few months, state and local
leaders from across the country are going

00:33:41.559 --> 00:33:43.549
to hold events to help get the word out.

00:33:43.549 --> 00:33:46.059
Go out there and join them.

00:33:46.059 --> 00:33:50.850
Secretary of Health and Human Services Kathleen
Sebelius is in Texas right now, working with

00:33:50.850 --> 00:33:54.309
folks on the ground to make sure this law
works for Texas families.

00:33:54.309 --> 00:33:56.559
All across the country, people are getting
ready.

00:33:56.559 --> 00:34:00.580
All kinds of people are working hand-in-hand
because we're all in this together -- that's

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:02.470
when America is at its best.

00:34:02.470 --> 00:34:04.149
That's what this country is all about.

00:34:04.149 --> 00:34:07.389
But we need you to spread the word.

00:34:07.389 --> 00:34:09.160
But you don't have to take my word for it.

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:12.440
If you talk to somebody who says, well, I
don't know, I was watching FOX News and they

00:34:12.440 --> 00:34:20.300
said this was horrible -- (laughter) -- you
can say, you know what, don't take my word

00:34:20.300 --> 00:34:25.050
for it, go on the website.

00:34:25.050 --> 00:34:28.679
See for yourself what the prices are.

00:34:28.679 --> 00:34:30.780
See for yourself what the choices are.

00:34:30.780 --> 00:34:34.580
Then make up your own mind.

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:36.100
That's all I'm asking.

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:37.300
Make up your own mind.

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:43.630
I promise you, if you go on the website and
it turns out you're going to save $100, $200,

00:34:43.630 --> 00:34:50.610
$300 a month on your insurance, or you'll
be able to buy insurance for the first time,

00:34:50.610 --> 00:34:55.330
even if you didn't vote for me -- (laughter)
-- I'll bet you'll sign up for that health

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:57.820
care plan.

00:34:57.820 --> 00:35:00.120
(Applause.)

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:16.470
So you don't need to listen to the politicians.

00:35:16.470 --> 00:35:17.470
You don't need to listen to me.

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:18.470
Just go check it out for yourself.

00:35:18.470 --> 00:35:20.390
Make up your own mind whether this works for
you.

00:35:20.390 --> 00:35:26.860
Look, part of the reason I need your help
to make this law work is because there are

00:35:26.860 --> 00:35:30.640
so many people out there working to make it
fail.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:37.060
One of the biggest newspapers in the country
recently published an editorial I thought

00:35:37.060 --> 00:35:38.060
was pretty good.

00:35:38.060 --> 00:35:43.590
They said, the Republicans in Congress are
poisoning Obamacare, then trying to claim

00:35:43.590 --> 00:35:47.260
it's sick.

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:50.760
(Laughter.)

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:51.920
That's exactly what's been happening.

00:35:51.920 --> 00:35:56.910
I mean, they have tried to put up every conceivable
roadblock.

00:35:56.910 --> 00:36:01.930
They cut funding for efforts to educate people
about what's in the law.

00:36:01.930 --> 00:36:05.940
Some of them said if their constituents called
them, we won't even try to explain to them

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:07.440
what's in the law.

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:12.950
They actually opened up an investigation into
people who try to help churches and charities

00:36:12.950 --> 00:36:18.730
understand how to help people sign up for
the law.

00:36:18.730 --> 00:36:24.520
Some of the tea party's biggest donors -- some
of the wealthiest men in America -- are funding

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:29.840
a cynical ad campaign trying to convince young
people not to buy health care at all.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:31.720
I mean, think about it.

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:35.380
These are billionaires several times over.

00:36:35.380 --> 00:36:37.580
You know they've got good health care.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:40.170
AUDIENCE: Right!

00:36:40.170 --> 00:36:41.470
(Applause.)

00:36:41.470 --> 00:36:47.580
THE PRESIDENT: But they are actually spending
money on television, trying to convince young

00:36:47.580 --> 00:36:52.050
people that if you've got the choice between
getting affordable health care or going without

00:36:52.050 --> 00:36:55.810
health care, you should choose not having
any health care.

00:36:55.810 --> 00:37:03.250
Now, do you think if you get sick or you get
hurt, and you get stuck with a massive bill,

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:06.880
these same folks, they're going to help you
out?

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:07.880
AUDIENCE: No!

00:37:07.880 --> 00:37:11.060
THE PRESIDENT: Are they going to pay for your
health care?

00:37:11.060 --> 00:37:13.870
AUDIENCE: No!

00:37:13.870 --> 00:37:20.261
THE PRESIDENT: It is interesting, though,
how over the last couple years, the Republican

00:37:20.261 --> 00:37:27.230
Party has just spun itself up around this
issue.

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:30.830
And the fact is the Republicans' biggest fear
at this point is not that the Affordable Care

00:37:30.830 --> 00:37:33.040
Act will fail.

00:37:33.040 --> 00:37:42.060
What they're worried about is it's going to
succeed.

00:37:42.060 --> 00:37:44.480
(Applause.)

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:45.520
I mean, think about it.

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:49.972
If it was as bad as they said it was going
to be, then they could just go ahead and let

00:37:49.972 --> 00:37:54.060
it happen and then everybody would hate it
so much, and then everybody would vote to

00:37:54.060 --> 00:37:57.660
repeal it, and that would be the end of it.

00:37:57.660 --> 00:38:00.570
So what is it that they're so scared about?

00:38:00.570 --> 00:38:03.690
AUDIENCE MEMBER: You!

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:06.810
(Laughter and applause.)

00:38:06.810 --> 00:38:14.500
THE PRESIDENT: They have made such a big political
issue out of this, trying to scare everybody

00:38:14.500 --> 00:38:22.630
with lies about "death panels" and "killing
granny" -- (laughter) -- right?

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:24.150
"Armageddon."

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:30.990
So if it actually works, they'll look pretty
bad.

00:38:30.990 --> 00:38:34.730
If it actually works, that will mean that
everything they were saying really wasn't

00:38:34.730 --> 00:38:36.980
true and they were just playing politics.

00:38:36.980 --> 00:38:38.650
AUDIENCE: That's right!

00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:44.170
THE PRESIDENT: Just the other day, one Republican
in Congress said we need to shut this thing

00:38:44.170 --> 00:38:51.160
down before the marketplaces open and people
get to see that they'll be getting coverage

00:38:51.160 --> 00:38:58.070
and getting these subsidies because -- and
I'm going to quote him here -- he said, "It's

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:01.470
going to prove almost impossible to undo Obamacare."

00:39:01.470 --> 00:39:03.220
(Laughter.)

00:39:03.220 --> 00:39:04.970
Right?

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:10.580
So in other words, we've got to shut this
thing down before people find out that they

00:39:10.580 --> 00:39:13.600
like it.

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:21.940
(Laughter and applause.)

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:23.460
That's a strange argument.

00:39:23.460 --> 00:39:26.570
Don't you think that's a strange argument?

00:39:26.570 --> 00:39:27.700
AUDIENCE: Yes!

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:30.930
THE PRESIDENT: And the closer we get, the
more desperate they get.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:38.460
I mean, over the last few weeks the rhetoric
has just been cranked up to a place I've never

00:39:38.460 --> 00:39:39.460
seen before.

00:39:39.460 --> 00:39:48.190
One congressman said that Obamacare is "the
most dangerous piece of legislation ever passed."

00:39:48.190 --> 00:39:49.650
(Laughter.)

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:51.110
Ever.

00:39:51.110 --> 00:39:58.600
In the history of America, this is the most
dangerous piece of legislation.

00:39:58.600 --> 00:39:59.920
(Laughter.)

00:39:59.920 --> 00:40:08.530
Creating a marketplace so people can buy group
insurance plans -- the most dangerous ever.

00:40:08.530 --> 00:40:15.930
You had a state representative somewhere say
that it's "as destructive to personal and

00:40:15.930 --> 00:40:19.110
individual liberty as the Fugitive Slave Act."

00:40:19.110 --> 00:40:24.850
AUDIENCE: Booo --

00:40:24.850 --> 00:40:26.740
THE PRESIDENT: Think about that.

00:40:26.740 --> 00:40:33.770
Affordable health care is worse than a law
that let slave owners get their runaway slaves

00:40:33.770 --> 00:40:35.110
back.

00:40:35.110 --> 00:40:37.810
AUDIENCE: No!

00:40:37.810 --> 00:40:38.850
THE PRESIDENT: I mean, these are quotes.

00:40:38.850 --> 00:40:44.220
I'm not making this stuff up.

00:40:44.220 --> 00:40:51.320
And here's one more that I've heard -- I like
this one -- we have to -- and I'm quoting

00:40:51.320 --> 00:40:59.760
here -- "We have to repeal this failure before
it literally kills women, kills children,

00:40:59.760 --> 00:41:02.390
kills senior citizens."

00:41:02.390 --> 00:41:07.850
Now, I have to say -- that one was from six
months ago -- I just want to point out we

00:41:07.850 --> 00:41:15.900
still have women -- (laughter) -- we still
have children, we still have senior citizens.

00:41:15.900 --> 00:41:18.930
(Applause.)

00:41:18.930 --> 00:41:30.310
All this would be funny if it wasn't so crazy.

00:41:30.310 --> 00:41:33.351
And a lot of it is just hot air.

00:41:33.351 --> 00:41:36.650
A lot of it is just politics.

00:41:36.650 --> 00:41:38.010
I understand that.

00:41:38.010 --> 00:41:44.440
But now the tea party Republicans have taken
it to a whole new level because they're threatening

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:50.840
either to shut down the government, or shut
down the entire economy by refusing to let

00:41:50.840 --> 00:41:56.960
America pay its bills for the first time in
history -- unless I agree to gut a law that

00:41:56.960 --> 00:41:58.530
will help millions of people.

00:41:58.530 --> 00:42:00.140
AUDIENCE: Booo --

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:04.030
THE PRESIDENT: Think about this.

00:42:04.030 --> 00:42:12.130
Shutting down the government just because
you don't like a law that was passed and found

00:42:12.130 --> 00:42:17.300
constitutional, and because you don't like
the idea of giving people new access to affordable

00:42:17.300 --> 00:42:24.320
health care -- what kind of idea is that?

00:42:24.320 --> 00:42:26.570
Think about how that would impact Maryland.

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:30.930
This is an area where lots of people would
be badly hurt by a government shutdown.

00:42:30.930 --> 00:42:36.020
A lot of people around here wake up and go
to serve their country every single day in

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:45.060
the federal government -- civilians who work
at military bases, analysts, scientists, janitors,

00:42:45.060 --> 00:42:47.770
people who process new veterans' and survivors'
benefit claims.

00:42:47.770 --> 00:42:52.560
They'd all have to stay home and not get paid.

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:58.510
And we all know it would badly damage the
economy.

00:42:58.510 --> 00:43:08.500
Whatever effect Obamacare might have on the
economy is far less than even a few days of

00:43:08.500 --> 00:43:09.500
government shutdown.

00:43:09.500 --> 00:43:10.500
(Applause.)

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:19.940
I mean, even if you believe that Obamacare
somehow was going to hurt the economy, it

00:43:19.940 --> 00:43:24.400
won't hurt the economy as bad as a government
shutdown.

00:43:24.400 --> 00:43:27.380
And by the way, the evidence is that it's
not going to hurt the economy.

00:43:27.380 --> 00:43:30.120
Obamacare is going to help the economy.

00:43:30.120 --> 00:43:34.520
And it's going to help families and help businesses.

00:43:34.520 --> 00:43:37.680
(Applause.)

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:46.680
As for not letting America pay its bills,
I have to say, no Congress before this one

00:43:46.680 --> 00:43:59.730
has ever -- ever -- in history been irresponsible
enough to threaten default, to threaten an

00:43:59.730 --> 00:44:10.310
economic shutdown, to suggest America not
pay its bills, just to try to blackmail a

00:44:10.310 --> 00:44:19.620
President into giving them some concessions
on issues that have nothing to do with a budget.

00:44:19.620 --> 00:44:24.310
I mean, this is the United States of America.

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:25.640
We're not a deadbeat nation.

00:44:25.640 --> 00:44:28.650
We don't run out on our tab.

00:44:28.650 --> 00:44:34.770
We don't not pay our note.

00:44:34.770 --> 00:44:43.150
We are the world's bedrock economy, the world's
currency of choice.

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:48.460
The entire world looks to us to make sure
that the world economy is stable.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:49.460
You don't mess with that.

00:44:49.460 --> 00:44:50.460
(Applause.)

00:44:50.460 --> 00:44:56.240
You don't mess with that.

00:44:56.240 --> 00:45:09.290
And that's why I will not negotiate on anything
when it comes to the full faith and credit

00:45:09.290 --> 00:45:12.550
of the United States of America.

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:14.500
(Applause.)

00:45:14.500 --> 00:45:23.210
We're not going to submit to this kind of
total irresponsibility.

00:45:23.210 --> 00:45:25.630
Congress needs to pay our bills on time.

00:45:25.630 --> 00:45:28.230
Congress needs to pass a budget on time.

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:31.850
Congress needs to put an end to governing
from crisis to crisis.

00:45:31.850 --> 00:45:32.850
(Applause.)

00:45:32.850 --> 00:45:37.280
Our focus as a country should be on creating
new jobs and growing our economy, and helping

00:45:37.280 --> 00:45:46.430
young people learn, and restoring security
for hardworking, middle-class families.

00:45:46.430 --> 00:45:48.620
(Applause.)

00:45:48.620 --> 00:45:52.780
This is not about the fortunes of any one
party.

00:45:52.780 --> 00:45:53.810
This is not about politics.

00:45:53.810 --> 00:45:57.800
This is about the future of our country.

00:45:57.800 --> 00:46:02.930
If Republicans do not like the law, they can
go through the regular channels and processes

00:46:02.930 --> 00:46:04.390
to try to change it.

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:09.270
That's why we have elections.

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:15.910
So they can go through the normal processes
and procedures of a democracy, but you do

00:46:15.910 --> 00:46:21.730
not threaten the full faith and credit of
the United States of America.

00:46:21.730 --> 00:46:23.860
(Applause.)

00:46:23.860 --> 00:46:29.830
And, meanwhile, we're going to keep implementing
the law.

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:31.900
AUDIENCE MEMBER: It's the law!

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:32.900
THE PRESIDENT: It's the law.

00:46:32.900 --> 00:46:35.281
And like I said, there are going to be some
glitches along the way.

00:46:35.281 --> 00:46:38.100
Every law has hiccups when it's first starting
off.

00:46:38.100 --> 00:46:43.960
People forget, by the way, Medicare Part D
-- passed by my predecessor, George Bush,

00:46:43.960 --> 00:46:48.670
passed by a Republican House of Representatives
-- the prescription drug bill passed into

00:46:48.670 --> 00:46:53.430
law 10 years ago was even more unpopular than
the Affordable Care Act before it took into

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:56.590
effect.

00:46:56.590 --> 00:46:58.380
Everybody was saying what a disaster it was
going to be.

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:06.560
The difference was Democrats worked with Republicans
to make it work even better.

00:47:06.560 --> 00:47:08.010
(Applause.)

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:12.350
Steny remembers this.

00:47:12.350 --> 00:47:18.080
Even though Democrats weren't happy that the
law wasn't paid for and was going to add hundreds

00:47:18.080 --> 00:47:22.950
of billions of dollars to the deficit, and
we weren't negotiating a better deal with

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:29.040
the drug companies, everybody worked -- once
it was the law -- to try to make it work.

00:47:29.040 --> 00:47:34.790
And today, about 90 percent of seniors like
their prescription drug coverage.

00:47:34.790 --> 00:47:41.960
So we may not get that same level of cooperation
from Republicans right now.

00:47:41.960 --> 00:47:46.830
But the good news is I believe eventually
they'll come around.

00:47:46.830 --> 00:47:51.220
Because Medicare and Social Security faced
the same kind of criticism.

00:47:51.220 --> 00:47:57.270
Before Medicare came into law, one Republican
warned that "one of these days, you and I

00:47:57.270 --> 00:48:01.300
are going to spend our sunset years telling
our children and our children's children what

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:07.370
it once was like in America when men were
free."

00:48:07.370 --> 00:48:10.720
That was Ronald Reagan.

00:48:10.720 --> 00:48:16.380
And eventually, Ronald Reagan came around
to Medicare and thought it was pretty good,

00:48:16.380 --> 00:48:19.190
and actually helped make it better.

00:48:19.190 --> 00:48:24.260
So that's what's going to happen with the
Affordable Care Act.

00:48:24.260 --> 00:48:31.440
And once it's working really well, I guarantee
you they will not call it Obamacare.

00:48:31.440 --> 00:48:37.200
(Laughter and applause.)

00:48:37.200 --> 00:48:50.500
Here is a prediction for you: A few years
from now, when people are using this to get

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:54.940
coverage and everybody is feeling pretty good
about all the choices and competition that

00:48:54.940 --> 00:49:00.850
they've got, there are going to be a whole
bunch of folks who say, yes, I always thought

00:49:00.850 --> 00:49:02.430
this provision was excellent.

00:49:02.430 --> 00:49:03.430
(Laughter.)

00:49:03.430 --> 00:49:08.510
I voted for that thing.

00:49:08.510 --> 00:49:11.710
You watch.

00:49:11.710 --> 00:49:14.790
(Laughter.)

00:49:14.790 --> 00:49:16.550
It will not be called Obamacare.

00:49:16.550 --> 00:49:17.550
(Laughter.)

00:49:17.550 --> 00:49:25.110
But I'm always willing to work with anybody
from either party.

00:49:25.110 --> 00:49:30.510
If you've got a serious idea for making the
Affordable Care Act better, or making our

00:49:30.510 --> 00:49:35.980
broader health care system better, I'm happy
to work with you -- because that's what the

00:49:35.980 --> 00:49:37.890
majority of the American people want.

00:49:37.890 --> 00:49:40.390
They don't want posturing; they want governing.

00:49:40.390 --> 00:49:45.570
They don't want politics; they want us to
work together to make the lives of ordinary

00:49:45.570 --> 00:49:51.570
Americans a little bit better, a little bit
more secure.

00:49:51.570 --> 00:49:53.750
(Applause.)

00:49:53.750 --> 00:50:03.510
So, Maryland, I'm asking for your help.

00:50:03.510 --> 00:50:05.910
(Applause.)

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:15.480
I need your help.

00:50:15.480 --> 00:50:17.880
(Applause.)

00:50:17.880 --> 00:50:20.770
We may have some very well-funded opponents.

00:50:20.770 --> 00:50:24.530
We may have some very talkative opponents.

00:50:24.530 --> 00:50:29.280
But you're going to be the best, most credible
messengers to spread the word about this law

00:50:29.280 --> 00:50:34.320
and all the benefits that the American people
stand to get and have earned.

00:50:34.320 --> 00:50:37.440
So tell your friends, tell your family.

00:50:37.440 --> 00:50:38.440
Get covered.

00:50:38.440 --> 00:50:39.830
Get on that website.

00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:45.030
Answer the questions of folks who don't know
what this is all about.

00:50:45.030 --> 00:50:47.310
Point them to HealthCare.gov.

00:50:47.310 --> 00:50:51.000
Teach them how to use the website.

00:50:51.000 --> 00:50:52.750
Make sure they sign up.

00:50:52.750 --> 00:50:55.070
Let's help our fellow Americans get covered.

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:56.070
(Applause.)

00:50:56.070 --> 00:50:59.230
Then let's keep on working to rebuild the
middle class.

00:50:59.230 --> 00:51:00.230
(Applause.)

00:51:00.230 --> 00:51:05.320
Let's go and focus on creating more good-paying
jobs.

00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:10.410
Let's build more ladders of opportunity for
everybody willing to work hard.

00:51:10.410 --> 00:51:11.410
(Applause.)

00:51:11.410 --> 00:51:18.210
Let's make sure the United States of America
keeps being a place where you can make it

00:51:18.210 --> 00:51:19.210
if you try.

00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:20.210
Thank you, everybody.

00:51:20.210 --> 00:51:21.210
God bless you.

00:51:21.210 --> 00:51:22.250
God bless the United States of America.

00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:22.510
(Applause.)

